From 761a4f98273295f662e591b78a0291e0fb72d0c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SriAkhilSJ <206201543+SriAkhilSJ@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 03:23:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(preview): reach the proxy from the preview window on
 first launch

Two faults that only appear once the IDE fork actually runs, both found by reasoning
about the real host rather than the lab.

1. CORS. The preview window is served from the app's own origin, which in Chromium is
   opaque (vscode-file://), so its registration POST to http://127.0.0.1:<port> is
   cross-origin. The browser preflights it (JSON body + custom header), and the proxy
   had no OPTIONS handler and no CORS headers, so the real request would never be sent:
   the window could only report "could not register this target".

   Adding CORS makes `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` necessary (an allow-list cannot
   tell the app's opaque origin apart from any sandboxed iframe), and `*` is what makes a
   registration SECRET necessary: without it, any page in the user's browser could
   register a file target and read it back through the proxy. So:

   - the proxy takes --secret, answers preflight, and refuses registration without it;
   - the 0600 endpoint file (src/preview/endpoint.py) carries the secret;
   - the workbench hands it to the preview window in that window's URL;
   - the lab issues its own secret and exercises the same gate.
   Capability, not origin, is what protects the server: a secret registers a target, a
   per-target token reads one, and a proxied page needs neither.

2. The pane's single-surface case. `show()` toggled `is-hidden` on the whole tab row,
   which also carries the new "a preview is available" chip: with the CopilotKit webview
   disabled, the Preview affordance vanished. Only the two tab buttons hide now.

Also, since this is the first pass where the window is created for real:

- the preview iframe mounts absolutely on the auxiliary window's body instead of
  overwriting the workbench's own container styles, which the workbench positions and
  lays out itself;
- the window keeps its NATIVE titlebar: our iframe covers the window's content area, and
  our chrome cannot drag a frameless window from inside a frame. Closing remains
  available from both the OS titlebar and the window's own Close button.

61 tests now (up from 57): the preflight answer, cross-origin read of the registration
reply, refusal with no/incorrect secret, the token-vs-secret separation, and the secret
pinned into the handshake file.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
